    prompt: 'The Roman emperor Marcus Aurelius wrote a note to himself to begin the morning by expecting ungrateful and difficult people. Is expecting a hard day gloomy, or wise?',
    perspectives: [
      { voice: 'A scientist', says: 'Surprise makes a bad moment feel bigger, because the body reacts before the mind catches up. A moment you saw coming leaves more room to think. That is why people rehearse fire drills before there is a fire.' },
      { voice: 'An artist', says: 'Actors rehearse the hardest scene the most, so it does not throw them on the night. Picture the tricky moment of your day before it comes. Then picture yourself handling it well.' },
      { voice: 'A grandparent of faith', says: 'Marcus did not stop at the warning. He wrote that such people act out of not knowing better, and that we are made to work together, like hands and feet. The point was to stay kind when the hard moment came.' },
      { voice: 'A skeptic', says: 'Expecting some difficulty is not the same as expecting the worst. Make a short list: what might go wrong today, and what would you do if it did? Then let the rest of the day surprise you.' },
    ],
