    perspectives: [
      { voice: 'A scientist', says: 'Steven Maier and Martin Seligman concluded that giving up under long, inescapable stress is the brain\'s default, not something learned. What gets learned is control: noticing that your actions make a difference switches the default off. The evidence comes from animal studies, and some researchers still question parts of it.' },
      { voice: 'An artist', says: 'The scientists who built the theory were the ones who rewrote it, half a century later. That takes a kind of courage most people never need. A changed mind, done honestly, is a masterpiece of its own.' },
      { voice: 'A grandparent of faith', says: 'Many traditions speak of hope as something practiced, not just felt. If giving up is the default under stress, then small acts of doing something are how hope is rebuilt. Start with one thing you can actually change today.' },
      { voice: 'A skeptic', says: 'Be careful with the leap from rats to people; the authors themselves were. But the practical idea is worth testing: after a failure, find one small action that clearly works. Notice what happens to your sense of what is possible.' },
    ],
    closing: 'After a hard failure, what is one small thing you can do that clearly works?',
